Q: Removing directory contents while ignoring a pattern in Python A: <p>You <em>could</em> (not necessarily <em>should</em>) achieve this using just the functionality available in the <code>pathlib</code> module as follows:</p>
<pre><code>from pathlib import Path


def remove(path: Path) -&gt; None:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    remove either a plain file or directory
    there is no equivalent to shutil.rmtree in the pathlib module
    hence the recursive approach shown here
    may not be as efficient as shutil.rmtree
    &quot;&quot;&quot;

    def _remove(path: Path) -&gt; None:
        for file in path.iterdir():
            if file.is_dir():
                _remove(file)
            else:
                file.unlink()
        path.rmdir()

    if path.is_dir():
        _remove(path)
    else:
        path.unlink()


def rm(path: Path, *patterns: str) -&gt; None:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    patterns are Unix style patterns - i.e., not to be confused with
    regular expressions (as defined in the re module)
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    for file in path.iterdir():
        if not any(file.match(p) for p in patterns):
            remove(file)


rm(Path(&quot;tmp&quot;), &quot;*.txt&quot;, &quot;*.tmp&quot;)
</code></pre>
